[Music]  so many believed that the digital realm  would be a place for hope and idealism  but as billion-dollar fines from the EU  testify the digital Giants have used  their monopoly power to generate ever  greater profits and squeeze out  competitors while at the same time  paying little or no tax does the  dominance of these new corporations  necessarily make them dangerous can we  break them up or regulate them to end  their monopoly or with plans for new  taxes failing is it already too late for  national governments to take control to  my left we've got Philip blond he is a  political philosopher anglican  theologian and his director of the race  pública think-tank moving to our rights  we have Anatole Colette ski he's an  economist and writer born in the Soviet  Union Anatole learned his trade at  Cambridge and Harvard he has since been  the times this economic editor and now  works as a columnist for Reuters and  last but definitely not least we have  Nicky Morgan she's Conservative MP and  former lawyer so the big question  reminds us all is does the dominance of  the digital giants necessarily make them  dangerous you have three minutes  undoubtedly it does one of the great  tragedies of the conservative ascendancy  over the past years in the UK is that we  haven't rethought competition law and  for a supposedly free-market party we  have presided over the most astonishing  market concentrations that we've  arguably ever witnessed it's far greater  than the Edwardian period for instance  and as a result we've introduced not  just oligopolies but monopolies and  tragically we don't recognize them as  such and part of that is because our  competition law is out of date and  actually runs on a pro monopoly model so  we have what's called consumer welfare  as the ultimate standard for assessing  market concentration but the point is is  if we have us one business that does  everything for  everyone always think Amazon's ambitions  that will deliver consumer welfare  because it has the economies of scale it  can deliver you things far cheaper than  anybody else and that's been our  competition law so consequently we've  let mergers through we've we've  celebrated what we should decry and we  are creating a new form of feudalism now  if you cast your mind back to the Middle  Ages as I know many of you do in the  Middle Ages the feudal serf relationship  was quite interesting serfs could  actually make a surplus they would work  the land and once they had paid the rent  to the Lord that surplus belonged to  them the modern feudalism that we are  now looking at is there was no surplus  at all for any workers that all the  returns from the undoubted innovations  technology brings goes to the owners of  that tech and they are creating a world  in which there is no asset for you and  because there is no asset or no  ownership for you because all of our  businesses are being put out of business  by the new monopolies there is no  conservative route to self emancipation  I believe that properly figured the  market economy will empower people  through ownership through business  ownership will be an agent of  redistribution that is now fanciful in  the face of the new monopolies and the  new politics in my view of the  emancipatory right if I can use that  conjunction has to be the restoration of  market power not just to consumers but  to the producers who are denied access  to the market and who are forever going  to be trapped in the new Ron ta economy  and will never own thank you  thank you very much Nichkhun over to you  well thank you very much and thank you  to the organizers opportunity to be here  to today and you mentioned I'm a a  lawyer so I shall do what lawyers always  do which is go back to definitions and  let's think what we mean by dangerous  what we mean by dangerous is something  that causes harm or has adverse  consequences now I'm not going to go in  quite the historical tour that Phillip  has just just done but I absolutely  agree with so much of what he has said  and last week in Parliament I'm part of  a group of conservative MPs who  relaunched one nation ISM we set out a  declaration of values and one things we  put in there about markets in the  economy was the need to break monopolies  and to enhance competition and I think  that the tech giants absolutely should  be front and center in terms of the way  that the government of the day my party  is thinking about this this issue and  there's a few things that really concern  me both as a former minister a chair of  the Treasury select committee but also  as a constituency member of parliament  the first undoubtedly is the financial  power of our tech giants just the size  of them and I was reading some this  morning that said Amazon the size of  Amazon the turnover is greater than the  size of example of the Portuguese  economy now you might not think the  Portuguese economy is one of those great  wonders of the world but when you've got  one company whose economy will Turner is  bigger than a country's and it becomes  harder and harder we're going to come  onto this to talk too about governments  being able to regulate and to to tackle  the next thing as undoubtedly the cyber  risks you know data is the new natural  resource and actually the risks whether  it's Cambridge analytic are the misuse  of data or the illegal harvesting of  data and the use of our data I don't  think we are nearly protective enough  about the data that we give to these  tech giants that they use for their own  commercial benefits you know a couple of  years ago Parliament email and many  other organizations emails have been  hacked and brought down now actually as  my colleague just Philip so wonderfully  said  whoever it was wanted to deal with our  constituency emails our potholes our  benefit queries and everything else for  a week fine they didn't want to do that  but the point is actually to bring down  large organizations including our  Parliament emails just shows the the  cyber risks that are out there and that  again are our tech giant's showing the  responsibility that they should do  there's a social power issue as well  when I was education secretary s but a  lot of my time thinking about well being  about mental health about character of  young people who want to develop if  we're having to give young people not  just young but often older but young  people lessons in how to deal with the  issues around social media the fact that  actually your friends online are not  necessarily friends in real life they're  not people who actually have their best  your best interests at heart then that  tells me something has gone wrong with  the tech that we are all using let alone  the fact we've also given a platform  these organizations are they are  publishers now that their platforms for  often illegal and extremely harmful  activity witnessed the recent  Christchurch killings where the killer  decided to broadcast live what he was  doing on Facebook and it took Facebook  far far too long which should me up  there in the first place but far far too  long to get it down as it was being  broadcast and then finally I think will  come on to the effects on democracy now  we see elections now consistently  undermined by what's going on online in  the UK obviously we've had the recent  online harms white paper we have the  Furman Review as well I think the area  of self-regulation is very much over but  there's no doubt that governments are  playing catch-up on this issue and  finally I think we just need to think  about this is reminiscent of the  financial crisis there was a big debate  about financial regulations or  light-touch self regulation and  governments caught up too late to the  dangers of that and we saw a financial  and banking crisis that we are still  seeing the effects of over 10 years  later but we shouldn't it's not all doom  and gloom they're all there are some  advantages there are some ways of  tackling we can talk perhaps about the  GDP our from the EU and some of the  regulations the Margaery  - Vesta Varga for example is taking a  relation to tackling competition but  there are probably in this audience and  elsewhere many startups charities and  others who benefit enormously from the  power of online the power to use the  Amazon Marketplace the power to  publicize through Facebook so the  questions how do you harness the  potential for good whilst also being  very realistic about the ability and the  desire to tackle the harms thanks so  much  Anatole well I agree with a lot of  pretty much everything we've just heard  from from the other two speakers with a  slight exception of a couple of points  that Philip made but I think we should  be careful not to overstate the case for  example I think the title of this talk  the new mafia I think is it gives an  false impression of the kind of danger  that society faces from these  organizations and also if we want to  find something constructive and positive  to do in response I think it's important  to distinguish between a number of  actual and potential harms all of which  require rather different responses from  Society for more debates talks and  interviews subscribe today to the  Institute of Arts and ideas at III TV  you

